  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nWarm front is moving northward across the region this afternoon, \nwith temps rising through the 70s and southerly winds gusting from \n20 to 35 mph for most areas east of the Missouri River. For tonight, \nsouthwesterly low-level jet at 925mb remains in place over the \neastern CWA. This will set the stage for some downslope winds off \nthe Coteau with gusts on the order of 30 to 40 mph. These are the \nspeeds the mean winds are showing in REFS, while 95th percentile \ngusts in REFS are closer to 40 mph along the ridge line and in the \nimmediate downslope area. Did do a blend of 50/50 NBM/NBM90th for \nwinds overnight in the Coteau/downslope region. Weak surface trough \nmoves through later tonight as well, with winds switching to the \nwest-northwest. CWA stays within the warm sector overnight, so \nlooking at mild overnight lows from the mid 40s to mid 50s. Did drop \nthe James valley a few degrees as models show this area in a lull \nwith wind speeds within the surface trough axis, so perhaps a longer \nperiod of lighter winds and better radiational cooling.\n\nRather warm air in place on Tuesday also, with 850mb temps from +17C \nto +20C across the CWA. Cold front will eventually move south \nthrough the area later in the day and into the evening, but before \nthen, high temperatures will be well above normal again and likely \nbe a few degrees either side of 80 degrees. Surface high pressure \nbuilds in from the northwest Tuesday night in the wake of the \npassing cold front. Winds go fairly light, but models indicate some \ndegree of mid and high level clouds. Did not drop lows too much \nbecause of the possible cloud cover, but it did seem like a \nsituation to at least drop lows a couple degrees below NBM in the \nJames valley. This surface high stays overhead all the way through \nThursday, keeping dry conditions in place and pleasant high \ntemperatures in the 60s and 70s.\n\nModels still showing another surge of warmth on Saturday as \nsoutherly low-level flow picks up again ahead of an approaching \nfrontal boundary. Grand Ensemble showing mean 850mb temps from +17C \nto +20C across the region once again, which is generally in the 80th \nto 90th percentile. Could see highs around 80 degrees or warmer on \nSaturday. Still decent model agreement in bringing a cold front \nthrough the region for the latter half of the weekend with cooler \ntemperatures moving in. Latest NBM temperatures bring 50s into the \nregion for highs Sunday and Monday, with lows starting to creep down \ninto the 30s by Monday night.",
